New firewall gateway certificate policy defaults
===============================================

New GatewayConnectionCreate and GatewayTestRequest bodies default verify_ssl
to true when omitted. Direct GatewayService creation and new ORM inserts use
the same default. Explicit false remains a deliberate compatibility opt-out.
Request null is invalid, not a synonym for disabling certificate verification.
Existing stored true/false values are not rewritten. Partial updates that omit
verify_ssl preserve the saved value; saved-gateway tests use the stored value
unless the caller supplies an explicit override, without persisting the override.

The ORM change is an application-side insert default, not a table-shape change
or data migration. Exported backups carry verify_ssl explicitly and retain that
choice on restore. Newly inserted records without any saved policy take the
new default; they do not silently acquire the legacy false value.

The default change is compatible with the verified Add Gateway UI. API clients
connecting to a self-signed device must provide appropriate trust or consciously
send false. Untrusted certificates are not retried with verification disabled.
Generated API default comments are refreshed from the matching source; public
exports regenerate after source hygiene so canonical/private audit comments
are not copied into the public contract.

Qualification limits: the real four-vendor TLS probe covers the normal HTTPS
443 configuration relocated to a private loopback peer, with synthetic appliance
responses. All four refused an untrusted certificate before authenticated HTTP
when verification was selected; trusted and explicit opt-out controls passed.
The probe closes its own transports. It does not establish standalone service
cleanup, custom-port protocol selection, all firmware interoperability or actual
hardware acceptance. Custom-port verification policy is now covered separately by
GATEWAY-HTTPS-POLICY: verified connections require HTTPS, including custom
ports. Standalone gateway test cleanup is covered by GATEWAY-TEST-LIFECYCLE
with explicit cancellation and best-effort close limits. This default change
does not globally change all low-level adapter defaults.
